+ ],
+ "image": null,
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Bangladesh's social protection system reaches about three in 10 households and is poorly set up to protect people from climate shocks, according to a report the Asian Development Bank (ADB) published on 18 November, Strengthening Adaptive Social Protection in Bangladesh.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The 52-page report found that 46.5% of beneficiaries did not meet the poverty criteria, while 71.0% of eligible families were excluded. Thirty-nine ministries and agencies run 115 safety net programmes, and key programmes provide less than 10.0% of the average income of poor and extremely poor households.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Social protection spending averaged 2.54% of GDP from 2015-16 to 2024-25. Without pensions and interest subsidies it was about 1.76% of GDP in 2024-25. About 10% of the 2024-25 social protection budget, over $722 million, went to disaster-related programmes.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The country faced 185 climate-related shocks from 2000 to 2019, and 58.3% of the population is exposed to climate hazards. Cyclones and floods could cost about $3.2 billion a year, about 2.2% of GDP. After Cyclone Aila, unemployment rose from 11% to 60% and the poverty headcount from 41% to 63%, the report says.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">It finds that a Tk 80 billion (about $650 million) contingency fund has been little used, and that the Ministry of Disaster Management and Relief's 2012 humanitarian guidelines do not allow money to be released before a shock. It recommends building adaptive social protection fully into the updated National Social Security Strategy, making anticipatory cash transfers routine, linking information systems with climate risk data, and setting up dedicated financing.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The lead authors are Shaila Rahman and Fazle Elahi Mahmud of Quay Asia, with management by Babken Babajanian of ADB. The report draws on a literature review and interviews with 26 people from government, development partners, UN agencies and civil society.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">NGOs run much of the anticipatory cash and early recovery work in Bangladesh, often with donor money because government rules do not allow payouts before a disaster. The report backs changing those rules and fixing targeting, both of which decide who gets help when the next cyclone hits.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether the updated National Social Security Strategy and the MODMR guidelines are revised to allow forecast-based transfers, and how the new Global Shield grants are linked to the safety net system.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.adb.org/publications/adaptive-social-protection-bangladesh-report\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Asian Development Bank: Strengthening Adaptive Social Protection in Bangladesh, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.adb.org/sites/default/files/publication/1175801/adaptive-social-protection-bangladesh-report.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Asian Development Bank: full report (PDF)</a></li>\n</ul>"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/alrd-angoc-land-food-sovereignty-conference-farmer-input-card.json b/src/data/posts/alrd-angoc-land-food-sovereignty-conference-farmer-input-card.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e61d6c1
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/alrd-angoc-land-food-sovereignty-conference-farmer-input-card.json
@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@
+{
+ "slug": "alrd-angoc-land-food-sovereignty-conference-farmer-input-card",
+ "date": "2026-11-18T23:15:00",
+ "modified": "2026-11-18T23:15:00",
+ "title": "Land rights groups make 33 demands; adviser cites Tk 2,500 farm card",
+ "excerpt": "At an ALRD and ANGOC conference, Rashed Al Mahmud Titumir said a Tk 2,500 input card for poor farmers would reduce food insecurity.",
+ "categories": [
+  "news",
+  "policy"
+ ],
+ "tags": [
+  "land",
+  "food-security",
+  "agriculture",
+  "food-security-agriculture",
+  "minority-development",
+  "women-empowerment"
+ ],
+ "image": null,
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A Tk 2,500 card for poor farmers to buy agricultural inputs will help reduce food insecurity, Dr Rashed Al Mahmud Titumir, Finance and Planning Adviser to the Prime Minister, said at the close of a two-day international conference in Dhaka on 17 November, Bonik Barta reported. He also said the government had started re-excavating 20,000 km of canals and rivers.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The conference, on responsible land governance and food sovereignty for climate-affected marginalised communities, was hosted by the Association for Land Reform and Development (ALRD) and the Asian NGO Coalition for Agrarian Reform and Rural Development (ANGOC). Civil society groups made 33 recommendations.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Speakers said land laws protecting smallholders, women and Indigenous peoples are weakly enforced and some are discriminatory. They included Khushi Kabir, chairperson of ALRD; Shamsul Huda, executive director of ALRD; Sabine Lauber, chief of mission of OHCHR; Syeda Rizwana Hasan, chief executive of BELA; and Mahin Sultan, senior fellow of practice at BRAC, the paper reported.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Land is at the root of poverty and climate displacement for many of the households NGOs work with. A farmer input card would be a new channel of support, and the conference set out an advocacy agenda for land and food rights groups.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether the input card is announced formally, who will qualify and how it will be distributed, and whether the 33 recommendations are taken up by the ministries.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://bonikbarta.com/bangladesh/K9CSpvBsFKceL6jK/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bonik Barta (Bangla): Land and food sovereignty conference ends, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/brick-kilns-phase-out-7-8-years-environment-minister-parliament.json b/src/data/posts/brick-kilns-phase-out-7-8-years-environment-minister-parliament.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..bbd712f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/brick-kilns-phase-out-7-8-years-environment-minister-parliament.json
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+{
+ "slug": "brick-kilns-phase-out-7-8-years-environment-minister-parliament",
+ "date": "2026-11-18T23:35:00",
+ "modified": "2026-11-18T23:35:00",
+ "title": "Government plans to phase out brick kilns within seven to eight years",
+ "excerpt": "Environment minister Abdul Awal Mintoo told parliament that kilns will be replaced by blocks. Drives since February shut 136 kilns.",
+ "categories": [
+  "news",
+  "policy"
+ ],
+ "tags": [
+  "environmental-conservation-and-sustainable-development",
+  "climate-change",
+  "health",
+  "poverty-reduction-decent-employment"
+ ],
+ "image": null,
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The government plans to phase out about 8,000 brick kilns gradually over the next seven to eight years and increase the production of environment-friendly blocks, Environment, Forest and Climate Change Minister Abdul Awal Mintoo told parliament on Wednesday (18 November), The Business Standard reported, citing BSS.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">He was replying to a call attention notice from Khairul Kabir Khokon, MP for Narsingdi-1. Between February and October this year, the minister said, authorities ran 205 mobile court drives, filed 435 cases, collected Tk 10.20 crore in fines and shut 136 kilns. The country has 31 air quality monitoring stations, he said.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In Bandarban, none of the district's 73 brick kilns has approval or environmental clearance, the Department of Environment told Bonik Barta in a report published on Wednesday. Reporters saw child labour at kilns in Faitong, Lama, and residents said kilns cut hills and dam canals. Mobile courts imposed fines of Tk 71.46 lakh between September and early November and sent arrest requests for 12 accused, the paper reported.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Brick kilns are a major source of winter air pollution and employ large numbers of seasonal workers, many of them in debt bondage to kiln owners. A phase-out affects health, environment and livelihood programmes at the same time.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether the plan comes with a timeline by district, support for kiln workers who lose seasonal work, and enforcement as the winter kiln season starts.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/bangladesh/govt-plans-phase-out-brick-kilns-within-7-8-years-increase-production-blocks-environment\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Business Standard (BSS): Govt plans to phase out brick kilns within 7-8 years, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://bonikbarta.com/bangladesh/9PlzJp6rzc97bCYW/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bonik Barta (Bangla): All 73 brick kilns in Bandarban illegal, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/cpd-garment-minimum-wage-range-tk-18578-24204.json b/src/data/posts/cpd-garment-minimum-wage-range-tk-18578-24204.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..74276c9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/cpd-garment-minimum-wage-range-tk-18578-24204.json
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+{
+ "slug": "cpd-garment-minimum-wage-range-tk-18578-24204",
+ "date": "2026-11-18T23:10:00",
+ "modified": "2026-11-18T23:10:00",
+ "title": "CPD proposes garment minimum wage of Tk 18,578 to Tk 24,204",
+ "excerpt": "CPD says the Tk 12,500 entry wage would need to be Tk 16,351 just to keep up with prices since 2023. Workers have asked for Tk 32,000.",
+ "categories": [
+  "research",
+  "news"
+ ],
+ "tags": [
+  "garments",
+  "poverty-reduction-decent-employment",
+  "livelihoods-economic-empowerment",
+  "research"
+ ],
+ "image": null,
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The garment sector's new minimum wage should be negotiated between a poverty-line wage of Tk 18,578 and a living wage of Tk 24,204 a month, the Centre for Policy Dialogue (CPD) said at a dialogue in Dhaka on Wednesday (18 November), Fair Wages in the Garment Sector 2026: Issues to Consider. The study team was led by Dr Nazneen Ahmed, executive director of CPD.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The current entry wage of Tk 12,500, in force since 1 December 2023, would need to be Tk 16,351 just to keep up with prices, CPD said, as the urban consumer price index rose 30.8% between November 2023 and October 2026. Workers proposed Tk 32,000 on 9 November.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">CPD put the monthly cost of a decent life for a family of four at Tk 38,243, assuming 1.58 earners per family. It also called for a permanent wage commission, a fixed, law-based schedule for setting wages with the next wage board formed by June 2029, and buyers taking part in wage talks.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The wage board is in session.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Garment workers' pay sets living standards for millions of families in Dhaka's industrial belt, where NGOs run health, child care and legal aid services. CPD's numbers give rights groups and the wage board an evidence-based range.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The wage board's proposal, the owners' response, and whether a new wage is announced before more factory unrest.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://cpd.org.bd/resources/2026/11/Determining-the-Minimum-Wage-in-the-RMG-Sector.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Centre for Policy Dialogue: Determining the Minimum Wage in the RMG Sector, 18 November 2026 (PDF)</a></li>\n</ul>"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/cyclone-akvan-severe-signal-3-chattogram-coxs-bazar-mongla-payra.json b/src/data/posts/cyclone-akvan-severe-signal-3-chattogram-coxs-bazar-mongla-payra.json
