  "title": "Land rights groups make 33 demands; adviser cites Tk 2,500 farm card",
- "excerpt": "At an ALRD and ANGOC conference, Rashed Al Mahmud Titumir said a Tk 2,500 input card for poor farmers would reduce food insecurity.",
+ "excerpt": "Rashed Al Mahmud Titumir told an ALRD and ANGOC conference that a Tk 2,500 farm card for poor farmers will end food insecurity.",
  "categories": [
   "news",
   "policy"
@@ -17,5 +17,5 @@
   "women-empowerment"
  ],
  "image": null,
- "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A Tk 2,500 card for poor farmers to buy agricultural inputs will help reduce food insecurity, Dr Rashed Al Mahmud Titumir, Finance and Planning Adviser to the Prime Minister, said at the close of a two-day international conference in Dhaka on 17 November, Bonik Barta reported. He also said the government had started re-excavating 20,000 km of canals and rivers.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The conference, on responsible land governance and food sovereignty for climate-affected marginalised communities, was hosted by the Association for Land Reform and Development (ALRD) and the Asian NGO Coalition for Agrarian Reform and Rural Development (ANGOC). Civil society groups made 33 recommendations.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Speakers said land laws protecting smallholders, women and Indigenous peoples are weakly enforced and some are discriminatory. They included Khushi Kabir, chairperson of ALRD; Shamsul Huda, executive director of ALRD; Sabine Lauber, chief of mission of OHCHR; Syeda Rizwana Hasan, chief executive of BELA; and Mahin Sultan, senior fellow of practice at BRAC, the paper reported.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Land is at the root of poverty and climate displacement for many of the households NGOs work with. A farmer input card would be a new channel of support, and the conference set out an advocacy agenda for land and food rights groups.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether the input card is announced formally, who will qualify and how it will be distributed, and whether the 33 recommendations are taken up by the ministries.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://bonikbarta.com/bangladesh/K9CSpvBsFKceL6jK/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bonik Barta (Bangla): Land and food sovereignty conference ends, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The government has introduced a farm card giving poor farmers Tk 2,500 to buy agricultural inputs, which will end food insecurity, Dr Rashed Al Mahmud Titumir, Finance and Planning Adviser to the Prime Minister, said, Bonik Barta reported. He joined online on the closing day of a two-day international conference on 17 November. He also said the government had started digging 20,000 km of canals and rivers.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The conference, on responsible land governance and food sovereignty for climate-affected marginalised communities, was hosted by the Association for Land Reform and Development (ALRD) and the Asian NGO Coalition for Agrarian Reform and Rural Development (ANGOC). Civil society groups made 33 recommendations.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In a session chaired by Mahin Sultan, senior fellow of practice at BRAC, speakers from Bangladesh, India, Indonesia and Nepal said laws protecting smallholders, women and Indigenous peoples exist but are poorly implemented, and some contain discriminatory provisions. Other participants included Khushi Kabir, chairperson of ALRD; Shamsul Huda, executive director of ALRD; Sabine Lauber, chief of mission of OHCHR; and Syeda Rizwana Hasan, chief executive of BELA, the paper reported.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Land is at the root of poverty and climate displacement for many of the households NGOs work with. The farm card is a new channel of support, and the conference set out an advocacy agenda for land and food rights groups.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Who qualifies for the farm card and how it is distributed, and whether the 33 recommendations are taken up by the ministries.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://bonikbarta.com/bangladesh/K9CSpvBsFKceL6jK/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bonik Barta (Bangla): Krishi card will end food insecurity, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
 }
diff --git a/src/data/posts/brick-kilns-phase-out-7-8-years-environment-minister-parliament.json b/src/data/posts/brick-kilns-phase-out-7-8-years-environment-minister-parliament.json
index bbd712f..94ab54d 100644
--- a/src/data/posts/brick-kilns-phase-out-7-8-years-environment-minister-parliament.json
