@@ -15,5 +15,5 @@
   "poverty-reduction-decent-employment"
  ],
  "image": null,
- "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The government plans to phase out about 8,000 brick kilns gradually over the next seven to eight years and increase the production of environment-friendly blocks, Environment, Forest and Climate Change Minister Abdul Awal Mintoo told parliament on Wednesday (18 November), The Business Standard reported, citing BSS.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">He was replying to a call attention notice from Khairul Kabir Khokon, MP for Narsingdi-1. Between February and October this year, the minister said, authorities ran 205 mobile court drives, filed 435 cases, collected Tk 10.20 crore in fines and shut 136 kilns. The country has 31 air quality monitoring stations, he said.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In Bandarban, none of the district's 73 brick kilns has approval or environmental clearance, the Department of Environment told Bonik Barta in a report published on Wednesday. Reporters saw child labour at kilns in Faitong, Lama, and residents said kilns cut hills and dam canals. Mobile courts imposed fines of Tk 71.46 lakh between September and early November and sent arrest requests for 12 accused, the paper reported.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Brick kilns are a major source of winter air pollution and employ large numbers of seasonal workers, many of them in debt bondage to kiln owners. A phase-out affects health, environment and livelihood programmes at the same time.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether the plan comes with a timeline by district, support for kiln workers who lose seasonal work, and enforcement as the winter kiln season starts.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/bangladesh/govt-plans-phase-out-brick-kilns-within-7-8-years-increase-production-blocks-environment\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Business Standard (BSS): Govt plans to phase out brick kilns within 7-8 years, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://bonikbarta.com/bangladesh/9PlzJp6rzc97bCYW/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bonik Barta (Bangla): All 73 brick kilns in Bandarban illegal, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The government plans to phase out about 8,000 brick kilns gradually over the next seven to eight years and increase the production of environment-friendly blocks, Environment, Forest and Climate Change Minister Abdul Awal Mintoo told parliament on Wednesday (18 November), The Business Standard reported, citing BSS.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">He was replying to a call attention notice from Khairul Kabir Khokon, MP for Narsingdi-1. Between February and October this year, the minister said, authorities ran 205 mobile court drives, filed 435 cases, collected Tk 10.20 crore in fines and shut 136 kilns. The country has 31 air quality monitoring stations, he said.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In Bandarban, none of the district's 73 brick kilns has approval or environmental clearance, the Department of Environment told Bonik Barta in a report published on Wednesday. Reporters saw child labour at kilns in Faitong, Lama, and residents said kilns cut hills and dam canals. Mobile courts imposed fines of Tk 71.46 lakh between September and early November and sent arrest requests for 12 accused, the paper reported.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Brick kilns are a major source of winter air pollution and employ large numbers of seasonal workers, many of them in debt bondage to kiln owners. A phase-out affects health, environment and livelihood programmes at the same time.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether the plan comes with a timeline by district, support for kiln workers who lose seasonal work, and enforcement as the winter kiln season starts.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/bangladesh/govt-plans-phase-out-brick-kilns-within-7-8-years-increase-production-blocks-environment\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Business Standard (BSS): Govt plans to phase out brick kilns within 7-8 years, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://bonikbarta.com/bangladesh/9PlzJp6rzc97bCYW/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bonik Barta (Bangla): Unauthorised brick kilns damaging hills and water bodies in Bandarban, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
 }
diff --git a/src/data/posts/cpd-garment-minimum-wage-range-tk-18578-24204.json b/src/data/posts/cpd-garment-minimum-wage-range-tk-18578-24204.json
index 74276c9..43a5115 100644
--- a/src/data/posts/cpd-garment-minimum-wage-range-tk-18578-24204.json
+++ b/src/data/posts/cpd-garment-minimum-wage-range-tk-18578-24204.json
@@ -15,5 +15,5 @@
   "research"
  ],
  "image": null,
- "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The garment sector's new minimum wage should be negotiated between a poverty-line wage of Tk 18,578 and a living wage of Tk 24,204 a month, the Centre for Policy Dialogue (CPD) said at a dialogue in Dhaka on Wednesday (18 November), Fair Wages in the Garment Sector 2026: Issues to Consider. The study team was led by Dr Nazneen Ahmed, executive director of CPD.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The current entry wage of Tk 12,500, in force since 1 December 2023, would need to be Tk 16,351 just to keep up with prices, CPD said, as the urban consumer price index rose 30.8% between November 2023 and October 2026. Workers proposed Tk 32,000 on 9 November.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">CPD put the monthly cost of a decent life for a family of four at Tk 38,243, assuming 1.58 earners per family. It also called for a permanent wage commission, a fixed, law-based schedule for setting wages with the next wage board formed by June 2029, and buyers taking part in wage talks.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The wage board is in session.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Garment workers' pay sets living standards for millions of families in Dhaka's industrial belt, where NGOs run health, child care and legal aid services. CPD's numbers give rights groups and the wage board an evidence-based range.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The wage board's proposal, the owners' response, and whether a new wage is announced before more factory unrest.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://cpd.org.bd/resources/2026/11/Determining-the-Minimum-Wage-in-the-RMG-Sector.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Centre for Policy Dialogue: Determining the Minimum Wage in the RMG Sector, 18 November 2026 (PDF)</a></li>\n</ul>"
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The garment sector's new minimum wage should be negotiated between a poverty-line wage of Tk 18,578 and a living wage of Tk 24,204 per earner a month, the Centre for Policy Dialogue (CPD) said in a presentation, Determining the Minimum Wage in the RMG Sector, in Dhaka on Wednesday (18 November). The study team was led by Dr Nazneen Ahmed, executive director of CPD.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The current entry wage of Tk 12,500, in force since 1 December 2023, would need to be Tk 16,351 just to keep up with prices, CPD said, as the urban consumer price index rose 30.8% between November 2023 and October 2026. Workers proposed Tk 32,000 at the wage board's second meeting on 9 November; owners have not submitted a proposal.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">CPD put the monthly cost of a decent life for a family of four at Tk 38,243, assuming 1.58 earners per family. It also called for a permanent wage commission, a fixed, law-based schedule for setting wages with the next wage board formed by June 2029, and buyers taking part in wage talks.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The wage board's third meeting, postponed on 16 November, has been rescheduled for 23 November.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Garment workers' pay sets living standards for millions of families in Dhaka's industrial belt, where NGOs run health, child care and legal aid services. CPD's numbers give rights groups and the wage board an evidence-based range.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The wage board's proposal, the owners' response, and whether a new wage is announced before more factory unrest.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://cpd.org.bd/resources/2026/11/Determining-the-Minimum-Wage-in-the-RMG-Sector.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Centre for Policy Dialogue: Determining the Minimum Wage in the RMG Sector, 18 November 2026 (PDF)</a></li>\n</ul>"
 }
diff --git a/src/data/posts/cyclone-akvan-severe-signal-3-chattogram-coxs-bazar-mongla-payra.json b/src/data/posts/cyclone-akvan-severe-signal-3-chattogram-coxs-bazar-mongla-payra.json
