@@ -14,5 +14,5 @@
   "health-support"
  ],
  "image": null,
- "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Hospitals admitted 1,674 dengue patients in the 24 hours to 8am on Wednesday (18 November), up from 1,537 a day earlier, according to Directorate General of Health Services (DGHS) figures reported by bdnews24.com's Bangla edition. One more death, in Dhaka division, took this year's toll to 508.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Dhaka division had the most new admissions (714), followed by Chattogram (311), Barishal (213), Khulna (190), Rajshahi (115), Rangpur (60), Mymensingh (50) and Sylhet (21).</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">So far this year, 167,318 people have been admitted with dengue and 161,193 have been discharged. 5,617 patients were still in hospital.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">State Minister for Health and Family Welfare Dr M A Muhit said on 17 November that all dengue tests are now free at government hospitals and 2,000 doctors have been trained, The Financial Express reported.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Admissions are rising again in mid-November, well past the usual peak, and cases are spread across every division. Health NGOs and community clinics are seeing the load outside Dhaka too.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Daily admissions over the next two weeks, as experts have warned of a hard December, and whether free testing reaches upazila level.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://bangla.bdnews24.com/bangladesh/55x4s6kje5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bdnews24.com (Bangla): Dengue: one death, 1,674 in hospital, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://today.thefinancialexpress.com.bd/metro-news/govt-hospitals-offering-all-dengue-tests-for-free-1794934610\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Financial Express: Govt hospitals offering all dengue tests for free, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Hospitals admitted 1,674 dengue patients in the 24 hours to 8am on Wednesday (18 November), according to Directorate General of Health Services (DGHS) figures reported by bdnews24.com's Bangla edition. A day earlier the figure was 1,537, The Financial Express reported. One more death, in Dhaka division, took this year's toll to 508.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Dhaka division had the most new admissions (714), followed by Chattogram (311), Barishal (213), Khulna (190), Rajshahi (115), Rangpur (60), Mymensingh (50) and Sylhet (21).</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">So far this year, 167,318 people have been admitted with dengue and 161,193 have been discharged. 5,617 patients were still in hospital.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">State Minister for Health and Family Welfare Dr M A Muhit told parliament on 17 November that all dengue tests are now free at government hospitals and 2,000 doctors have been trained, The Financial Express reported.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Admissions are rising again in mid-November, well past the usual peak, and cases are spread across every division. Health NGOs and community clinics are seeing the load outside Dhaka too.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Daily admissions over the next two weeks, as experts have warned of a hard December, and whether free testing reaches upazila level.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://bangla.bdnews24.com/bangladesh/55x4s6kje5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bdnews24.com (Bangla): Dengue: one death, 1,674 in hospital, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://today.thefinancialexpress.com.bd/metro-news/govt-hospitals-offering-all-dengue-tests-for-free-1794934610\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Financial Express: Govt hospitals offering all dengue tests for free, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
 }
diff --git a/src/data/posts/global-shield-climate-risk-grants-bangladesh-30-million-euros.json b/src/data/posts/global-shield-climate-risk-grants-bangladesh-30-million-euros.json
index 22a1748..ff20eb0 100644
--- a/src/data/posts/global-shield-climate-risk-grants-bangladesh-30-million-euros.json
+++ b/src/data/posts/global-shield-climate-risk-grants-bangladesh-30-million-euros.json
@@ -16,5 +16,5 @@
   "social-protection"
  ],
  "image": null,
- "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Bangladesh will receive at least €30 million (about Tk 415 crore) in grants from the Global Shield against Climate Risks, Dr Md Saimum Parvez, Special Assistant to the Prime Minister on Environment, Forest and Climate Change, said at the Bangladesh Pavilion at COP31 in Antalya on Wednesday (18 November), The Business Standard reported, citing BSS.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">\"This is entirely a grant, not a loan,\" he said. The application was made through the Economic Relations Division (ERD).</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The money will pay for flood and cyclone risk assessment, better data systems, climate insurance for farmers, fishers and small entrepreneurs, and fast assistance released when forecasts show a disaster is coming. A further $20 million (about Tk 250 crore) could follow if the first phase is carried out well.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Bangladesh has also been chosen to prepare an investment plan of up to $30 million under the Climate Investment Funds' ARISE programme, the report said. The Global Shield was set up by the G7 and the V20 group of climate-vulnerable countries.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Bangladesh has pressed at COP31 for climate finance as grants rather than loans. This is grant money for exactly the work many NGOs already do: early warning, forecast-based cash, and insurance for poor households. How it is channelled will decide whether community organisations get a share.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Which agencies and partners will deliver the programmes, whether NGOs and local groups have a role in forecast-based payouts, and when the first money arrives.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/bangladesh/bangladesh-receive-tk415cr-global-shield-grants-saimum-parvez-1606216\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Business Standard (BSS): Bangladesh to receive Tk415cr Global Shield grants, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Bangladesh will receive at least €30 million (about Tk 415 crore) in grants from the Global Shield against Climate Risks, Dr Md Saimum Parvez, Special Assistant to the Prime Minister on Environment, Forest and Climate Change, said at the Bangladesh Pavilion at COP31 in Antalya on Wednesday (18 November), The Business Standard reported, citing BSS.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">\"This is entirely a grant, not a loan,\" he said. The application was made through the Economic Relations Division (ERD).</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The money will pay for flood and cyclone risk assessment, better data systems, climate insurance for farmers, fishers and small entrepreneurs, and fast assistance released when forecasts show a disaster is coming. A further $20 million (about Tk 250 crore) could follow if the first phase is carried out well.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Separately, at a Mangrove Alliance for Climate ministerial meeting, he said Bangladesh has been chosen to prepare an investment plan of up to $30 million under the Climate Investment Funds' ARISE programme, the report said. The Global Shield was set up by the G7 and the V20 group of climate-vulnerable countries.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This is grant money for exactly the work many NGOs already do: early warning, forecast-based cash, and insurance for poor households. How it is channelled will decide whether community organisations get a share.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Which agencies and partners will deliver the programmes, whether NGOs and local groups have a role in forecast-based payouts, and when the first money arrives.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/bangladesh/bangladesh-receive-tk415cr-global-shield-grants-saimum-parvez-1606216\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Business Standard (BSS): Bangladesh to receive Tk415cr Global Shield grants, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
 }
diff --git a/src/data/posts/hrss-111-people-faced-action-expressing-views-january-october-2026.json b/src/data/posts/hrss-111-people-faced-action-expressing-views-january-october-2026.json
index bba023a..cb801da 100644
--- a/src/data/posts/hrss-111-people-faced-action-expressing-views-january-october-2026.json
+++ b/src/data/posts/hrss-111-people-faced-action-expressing-views-january-october-2026.json
@@ -16,5 +16,5 @@
   "legal-aid-and-access-to-justice"
  ],
  "image": null,
- "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">At least 111 people faced legal action, arrest, attacks or other reprisals for expressing their views between January and October 2026, the Human Rights Support Society (HRSS) said on Wednesday (18 November), The Daily Star reported. Ijajul Islam, executive director of HRSS, presented the findings at a roundtable at the Jatiya Press Club.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Of the 111, 62 people (55.9%) were targeted for criticising the Prime Minister, his family, the government or BNP leaders. HRSS recorded 86 incidents in 38 districts. Sixty-six people were arrested or detained: 47 were shown arrested in cases and 19 were held without a case.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Of 66 legal actions, 36 were under cyber laws, 13 were for defamation or under the Penal Code, and 10 were under the Anti-Terrorism Act. Twenty-five of those targeted were journalists; cases were filed against 23 and four were arrested. Two people were beaten to death, and eight lost jobs or places in education.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">HRSS also warned that the proposed amendments to the cyber protection law could restrict expression, privacy and journalism.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Civic space shapes what NGOs, researchers and community groups can safely say. The figures arrive while the government is taking public comments on its revised cyber law draft, which closes on 25 November.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether the final cyber law draft narrows the offences HRSS flags, and whether rights groups and NGO networks send joint comments before the deadline.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/rights/news/111-face-action-over-expressing-views-10-months-hrss-4327116\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Daily Star: 111 face action over expressing views in 10 months: HRSS, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">At least 111 people faced legal action, arrest, attacks or other reprisals for expressing their views between January and October 2026, the Human Rights Support Society (HRSS) said on Wednesday (18 November), The Daily Star reported. Ijajul Islam, executive director of HRSS, presented the findings at a roundtable at the Jatiya Press Club.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Of the 111, 62 people (55.9%) were targeted for criticising the Prime Minister, his family, the government or BNP leaders. HRSS recorded 86 incidents in 38 districts. Sixty-six people were arrested or detained: 47 were shown arrested in cases and 19 were held without a case.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Of 66 legal actions, 36 were under cyber laws, 13 were for defamation or under the Penal Code, and 10 were under the Anti-Terrorism Act. Of the 84 people whose professions could be identified, 25 were journalists; cases were filed against 23 and four were arrested. Two people were beaten to death, and eight lost jobs or places in education.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">HRSS also warned that the proposed amendments to the cyber protection law could restrict expression, privacy and journalism.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Civic space shapes what NGOs, researchers and community groups can safely say. The figures arrive while the government's revised cyber law draft is open for public comment.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether the final cyber law draft narrows the offences HRSS flags, and whether rights groups and NGO networks send joint comments before the deadline.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/rights/news/111-face-action-over-expressing-views-10-months-hrss-4327116\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Daily Star: 111 face action over expressing views in 10 months: HRSS, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
 }
