-+ ],
-+ "image": null,
-+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Wage Earners' Welfare Board (WEWB) cannot cash Tk 5.64 billion (Tk 564 crore) of matured fixed deposits now held by the merged Sammilito Islami Bank, and has asked the government to step in, The Financial Express reported on Wednesday (18 November).</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The deposits were placed with four banks that were merged into Sammilito Islami Bank: First Security Islami Bank (Tk 4.45 billion), Union Bank (Tk 1.05 billion), Global Islami Bank (Tk 130 million) and Exim Bank (Tk 10 million).</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The board says the frozen money is hampering welfare services for migrant workers and their families. The Financial Institutions Division said it is working to resolve the issue, the report said. No officials were named.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The WEWB fund comes from migrant workers' own fees and pays for services such as support to families, scholarships and help with repatriating bodies. Organisations working with migrants will see the effect if payments slow.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether the Financial Institutions Division releases the money, and whether any WEWB services are delayed or cut in the meantime.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://today.thefinancialexpress.com.bd/trade-market/wewb-seeks-govt-intervention-to-encash-tk-564b-fdrs-with-sammilito-islami-bank-1794932742\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Financial Express: WEWB seeks govt intervention to encash Tk 5.64b FDRs with Sammilito Islami Bank, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
-+}
