+ ],
+ "image": null,
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The High Court on Wednesday (18 November) stopped all public university admission tests until 31 December, bdnews24.com reported. A bench of Justice Ahmed Sohel and Justice SM Iftekhar Uddin Mahmud gave the order after a preliminary hearing on a writ petition filed by lawyer Kamrun Nahar Mahmud.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The petition says students who failed two subjects in this year's HSC exams are being shut out of admission tests. It also challenges universities for ignoring a University Grants Commission directive to start tests on 1 January. About 450,000 students failed two subjects, bdnews24.com's Bangla edition reported.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Deputy Attorney General Mohammad Mohsin Kabir confirmed the order and said the government would hold retests within a month for students who failed.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The ruling affects the timing of university entry for hundreds of thousands of young people, and a fairer chance for those who failed two subjects. Education and youth NGOs that support first-generation and low-income students may need to adjust coaching, scholarship and counselling plans.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The date of the HSC retests, a full hearing on the petition, and new admission schedules from the universities.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://bdnews24.com/education/aaz0rfzwsk\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bdnews24.com: High Court halts public university admission tests, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://bangla.bdnews24.com/bangladesh/5k3vfbp7nj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bdnews24.com (Bangla): Admission tests suspended until 31 December, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/icj-rohingya-genocide-verdict-today-dhaka-seeks-accountability-repatriation.json b/src/data/posts/icj-rohingya-genocide-verdict-today-dhaka-seeks-accountability-repatriation.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..884d954
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/icj-rohingya-genocide-verdict-today-dhaka-seeks-accountability-repatriation.json
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+{
+ "slug": "icj-rohingya-genocide-verdict-today-dhaka-seeks-accountability-repatriation",
+ "date": "2026-11-19T07:30:00",
+ "modified": "2026-11-19T07:30:00",
+ "title": "ICJ rules on Rohingya genocide today as Dhaka seeks accountability",
+ "excerpt": "The world court gives its verdict in The Gambia v Myanmar at 3pm Bangladesh time. Refugees in Cox's Bazar say they want justice and a safe way home.",
+ "categories": [
+  "news"
+ ],
+ "tags": [
+  "humanitarian-rohingya-response",
+  "rohingya-response",
+  "coxs-bazar",
+  "human-rights",
+  "governance-human-rights"
+ ],
+ "image": null,
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Court of Justice (ICJ) will deliver its judgment in The Gambia's genocide case against Myanmar today, Thursday 19 November. The reading is due to start at 10am in The Hague, which is 3pm in Bangladesh, bdnews24.com's Bangla edition reported. On the eve of the ruling, State Minister for Foreign Affairs Shama Obaed Islam said accountability is \"essential to delivering justice to the Rohingya people\", The Business Standard reported, citing UNB.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">She made the remarks on 18 November when she met Kelley Currie, the UN Special Rapporteur on the situation of human rights in Myanmar, at the foreign ministry. She said Bangladesh is following the judgment closely, called repatriation \"the only viable and durable solution\", and urged a coordinated international roadmap for return. She put the number of Rohingya refugees in Bangladesh at about 1.3 million.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The case</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Gambia filed the case in November 2019 under the Genocide Convention, with the backing of the 57-member Organisation of Islamic Cooperation, bdnews24.com's Bangla edition reported. Eleven countries have intervened. Hearings on the merits ended in January 2026. The court will decide whether Myanmar as a state broke the convention. It is not a criminal trial of individuals, and the judgment is final, with no appeal, Human Rights Watch (HRW) has noted. The court has no power of its own to enforce it.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Voices from the camps</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Refugees in Cox's Bazar told bdnews24.com they want justice and repatriation. \"We don't want revenge; we want justice,\" said Rahima Khushi, a Rohingya rights worker who has interpreted for The Gambia's legal team. Refugee Relief and Repatriation Commissioner Mizanur Rahman told the paper the ruling could strengthen international pressure on Myanmar.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">\"We want to go back,\" Muhammad Zubair, chairman of the Arakan Rohingya Society for Peace and Human Rights, told Al Jazeera. Thomas Kean of the International Crisis Group warned that \"the Arakan Army can hardly make guarantees around safety while a war is going on\" in Rakhine State.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This is the biggest accountability moment for the Rohingya since the 2017 exodus, when more than 700,000 people fled to Bangladesh. A ruling against Myanmar would give Bangladesh and refugee groups stronger grounds to press for safe return, but it will not change daily life in the camps, where food aid is funded only to April 2027. NGOs should expect gatherings in the camps today and many questions from refugees about what the verdict means for them.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The judgment this afternoon, Myanmar's response, statements from donors and the UN Security Council, and whether the verdict brings new funding pledges for the 2027 response.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/foreign-policy/dhaka-stresses-accountability-rohingya-atrocities-ahead-icj-judgment-1606761\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Business Standard (UNB): Dhaka stresses accountability for Rohingya atrocities ahead of ICJ judgment, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://bangla.bdnews24.com/samagrabangladesh/b0y9576702\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bdnews24.com (Bangla): Rohingya await ICJ verdict, 19 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2026/11/18/we-want-to-go-back-rohingya-refugees-pin-hopes-on-icj-genocide-verdict\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Al Jazeera: 'We want to go back': Rohingya refugees pin hopes on ICJ genocide verdict, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.hrw.org/news/2026/11/03/questions-and-answers-international-court-of-justice-judgment-in-myanmar-genocide\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Human Rights Watch: Questions and answers on the ICJ judgment in the Myanmar genocide case, 3 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/mohakhali-sattola-slum-fire-100-families-homeless.json b/src/data/posts/mohakhali-sattola-slum-fire-100-families-homeless.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..629f158
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/mohakhali-sattola-slum-fire-100-families-homeless.json
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+{
+ "slug": "mohakhali-sattola-slum-fire-100-families-homeless",
+ "date": "2026-11-19T06:30:00",
+ "modified": "2026-11-19T06:30:00",
+ "title": "Fire leaves over 100 families homeless in Mohakhali's Sattola slum",
+ "excerpt": "It is at least the eighth fire in the slum since 2012. Residents blame illegal power and gas lines, and some say fires have been set over land disputes.",
+ "categories": [
+  "news"
+ ],
+ "tags": [
+  "man-made-disaster",
+  "shelter",
+  "housing-and-community-amenities",
+  "humanitarian-response",
+  "poverty"
+ ],
+ "image": null,
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A fire on Wednesday morning (18 November) left more than 100 families homeless in the Sattola slum in Mohakhali, Dhaka, bdnews24.com's Bangla edition reported. Eight fire service units fought the blaze. The first arrived at 10:10am and the fire was brought under control at 11:15am. No one was reported hurt.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Lt Col Mohammad Mahmudul Hasan, Director (Operations and Maintenance) of the Fire Service, said the cause was not yet known and an investigation committee would be formed.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">A slum that keeps burning</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Sattola also caught fire in 2012, 2015, 2016, 2020, 2021 and 2025, the paper said. It pointed to illegal electricity and gas connections, and to residents' claims that some fires have been set on purpose in disputes over the land. The report did not mention any relief for the families.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Families who lose their homes in slum fires often lose savings, documents and school materials too, and many rent rather than own, so they get little help. Urban poverty, shelter and child protection NGOs in Dhaka may be needed for emergency support, and the repeated fires point to unsafe wiring and insecure tenure that city authorities have not fixed.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Relief from the city corporation or district administration, the findings of the investigation committee, and whether families are allowed to rebuild.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://bangla.bdnews24.com/bangladesh/vm7etn0bmg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bdnews24.com (Bangla): Fire at Mohakhali Sattola slum, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/secondary-education-project-revision-laptops-e-buses-library-grants-cut.json b/src/data/posts/secondary-education-project-revision-laptops-e-buses-library-grants-cut.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..0511315
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/secondary-education-project-revision-laptops-e-buses-library-grants-cut.json
@@ -0,0 +1,18 @@
+{
+ "slug": "secondary-education-project-revision-laptops-e-buses-library-grants-cut",
+ "date": "2026-11-19T07:00:00",
+ "modified": "2026-11-19T07:00:00",
+ "title": "Secondary education project plan swaps library money for laptops",
+ "excerpt": "A revised World Bank-funded project would give laptops to 30,000 top SSC students and cut library grants by about half. The bank must agree.",
+ "categories": [
+  "news"
+ ],
+ "tags": [
+  "education",
+  "education-and-human-resource-development",
+  "digital-development",
+  "aid-financing"
+ ],
+ "image": null,
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A revised plan for a World Bank-funded secondary education project would spend Tk 900 crore on laptops for top SSC students, 100 pilot electric buses and solar panels at 2,000 schools and madrasahs, The Business Standard reported on 18 November. The plan cuts library grants and stops central book buying to save money.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Key figures</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Project cost would rise from Tk 3,304.08 crore to Tk 3,659.75 crore</li>\n<li>Laptops for 30,000 students who got GPA-5, at Tk 1 lakh each</li>\n<li>Library grants would fall from Tk 118.48 crore to Tk 60 crore</li>\n<li>The project covers 29,621 institutions and is backed by $300 million from the World Bank's International Development Association (IDA)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The project's targets for 2028 include 65% of Grade 8 students reaching minimum proficiency in Bangla and 90% in maths, and 74% of students staying in school through Grade 10. The Economic Relations Division is reviewing the proposal, and it needs the World Bank's consent.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The change moves donor money for secondary schools away from libraries, which every pupil can use, towards devices for the highest scorers. Education NGOs that work on learning poverty, girls' retention and rural schools may want to ask how the new items will help the project meet its own learning and retention targets.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether the World Bank agrees to the revision, and whether the government sets out how the laptops and e-buses link to the project's results.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/bangladesh/ssc-toppers-get-laptops-schools-get-e-buses-solar-panels-1606041\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Business Standard: SSC toppers to get laptops, schools to get e-buses, solar panels, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/special-solar-power-installation-bill-2026-passed-farmland-tender-concerns.json b/src/data/posts/special-solar-power-installation-bill-2026-passed-farmland-tender-concerns.json
new file mode 100644
