+ "modified": "2026-11-19T08:05:00",
  "title": "ICJ rules on Rohingya genocide today as Dhaka seeks accountability",
- "excerpt": "The world court gives its verdict in The Gambia v Myanmar at 3pm Bangladesh time. Refugees in Cox's Bazar say they want justice and a safe way home.",
+ "excerpt": "The world court gives its verdict in The Gambia v Myanmar later today. Refugees in Cox's Bazar say they want justice and a safe way home.",
  "categories": [
   "news"
  ],
@@ -15,5 +15,5 @@
   "governance-human-rights"
  ],
  "image": null,
- "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Court of Justice (ICJ) will deliver its judgment in The Gambia's genocide case against Myanmar today, Thursday 19 November. The reading is due to start at 10am in The Hague, which is 3pm in Bangladesh, bdnews24.com's Bangla edition reported. On the eve of the ruling, State Minister for Foreign Affairs Shama Obaed Islam said accountability is \"essential to delivering justice to the Rohingya people\", The Business Standard reported, citing UNB.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">She made the remarks on 18 November when she met Kelley Currie, the UN Special Rapporteur on the situation of human rights in Myanmar, at the foreign ministry. She said Bangladesh is following the judgment closely, called repatriation \"the only viable and durable solution\", and urged a coordinated international roadmap for return. She put the number of Rohingya refugees in Bangladesh at about 1.3 million.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The case</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Gambia filed the case in November 2019 under the Genocide Convention, with the backing of the 57-member Organisation of Islamic Cooperation, bdnews24.com's Bangla edition reported. Eleven countries have intervened. Hearings on the merits ended in January 2026. The court will decide whether Myanmar as a state broke the convention. It is not a criminal trial of individuals, and the judgment is final, with no appeal, Human Rights Watch (HRW) has noted. The court has no power of its own to enforce it.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Voices from the camps</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Refugees in Cox's Bazar told bdnews24.com they want justice and repatriation. \"We don't want revenge; we want justice,\" said Rahima Khushi, a Rohingya rights worker who has interpreted for The Gambia's legal team. Refugee Relief and Repatriation Commissioner Mizanur Rahman told the paper the ruling could strengthen international pressure on Myanmar.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">\"We want to go back,\" Muhammad Zubair, chairman of the Arakan Rohingya Society for Peace and Human Rights, told Al Jazeera. Thomas Kean of the International Crisis Group warned that \"the Arakan Army can hardly make guarantees around safety while a war is going on\" in Rakhine State.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This is the biggest accountability moment for the Rohingya since the 2017 exodus, when more than 700,000 people fled to Bangladesh. A ruling against Myanmar would give Bangladesh and refugee groups stronger grounds to press for safe return, but it will not change daily life in the camps, where food aid is funded only to April 2027. NGOs should expect gatherings in the camps today and many questions from refugees about what the verdict means for them.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The judgment this afternoon, Myanmar's response, statements from donors and the UN Security Council, and whether the verdict brings new funding pledges for the 2027 response.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/foreign-policy/dhaka-stresses-accountability-rohingya-atrocities-ahead-icj-judgment-1606761\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Business Standard (UNB): Dhaka stresses accountability for Rohingya atrocities ahead of ICJ judgment, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://bangla.bdnews24.com/samagrabangladesh/b0y9576702\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bdnews24.com (Bangla): Rohingya await ICJ verdict, 19 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2026/11/18/we-want-to-go-back-rohingya-refugees-pin-hopes-on-icj-genocide-verdict\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Al Jazeera: 'We want to go back': Rohingya refugees pin hopes on ICJ genocide verdict, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.hrw.org/news/2026/11/03/questions-and-answers-international-court-of-justice-judgment-in-myanmar-genocide\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Human Rights Watch: Questions and answers on the ICJ judgment in the Myanmar genocide case, 3 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The International Court of Justice (ICJ) will deliver its judgment in The Gambia's genocide case against Myanmar today, Thursday 19 November, at a public sitting in The Hague. On the eve of the ruling, State Minister for Foreign Affairs Shama Obaed Islam said accountability is \"essential to delivering justice to the Rohingya people\", The Business Standard reported, citing UNB.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">She made the remarks on 18 November when she met Kelley Currie, the UN Special Rapporteur on the situation of human rights in Myanmar, at the foreign ministry. She said Bangladesh is following the judgment closely, called repatriation \"the only viable and durable solution\", and urged a coordinated international roadmap for return. She put the number of Rohingya refugees in Bangladesh at about 1.3 million.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The case</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Gambia filed the case in November 2019 under the Genocide Convention, with the backing of the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation, Human Rights Watch (HRW) says. Eleven countries have been allowed to intervene. Hearings on the merits ended in January 2026. The court will decide whether Myanmar as a state broke the convention. It is not a criminal trial of individuals, and the judgment is final, with no appeal, HRW has noted. The court has no power of its own to enforce it.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Voices from the camps</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Refugees in Cox's Bazar told bdnews24.com they want justice and repatriation. \"We don't want revenge; we want justice,\" said Rahima Khushi, a Rohingya rights worker who has interpreted for The Gambia's legal team. Refugee Relief and Repatriation Commissioner Mizanur Rahman told the paper the ruling could strengthen international pressure on Myanmar.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">\"We want to go back,\" Mohammad Zubair, chairman of the Arakan Rohingya Society for Peace and Human Rights, told Al Jazeera. Thomas Kean of the International Crisis Group warned that \"the Arakan Army can hardly make guarantees around safety while a war is going on\" in Rakhine State.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This is the biggest accountability moment for the Rohingya since the 2017 exodus, when more than 700,000 people fled to Bangladesh. A ruling against Myanmar would give Bangladesh and refugee groups stronger grounds to press for safe return, but it will not by itself change daily life in the camps, where aid budgets are already shrinking. NGOs should expect gatherings in the camps today and many questions from refugees about what the verdict means for them.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The judgment itself, Myanmar's response, statements from donors and the UN Security Council, and whether the verdict brings new funding pledges for the 2027 response.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/foreign-policy/dhaka-stresses-accountability-rohingya-atrocities-ahead-icj-judgment-1606761\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Business Standard (UNB): Dhaka stresses accountability for Rohingya atrocities ahead of ICJ judgment, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://bangla.bdnews24.com/samagrabangladesh/b0y9576702\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bdnews24.com (Bangla): Rohingya await ICJ verdict, 19 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2026/11/18/we-want-to-go-back-rohingya-refugees-pin-hopes-on-icj-genocide-verdict\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Al Jazeera: 'We want to go back': Rohingya refugees pin hopes on ICJ genocide verdict, 18 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.hrw.org/news/2026/11/03/questions-and-answers-international-court-of-justice-judgment-in-myanmar-genocide\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Human Rights Watch: Questions and answers on the ICJ judgment in the Myanmar genocide case, 3 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
 }
